		<section class="mt-8 max-w-4xl rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-6">
			<h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-foreground md:text-3xl">Common Mistakes New Skill Game Players Make</h2>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
				These are the patterns most consistently observed in newer competitive players. Recognising
				them early can save months of avoidable loss.
			</p>
			<ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-4 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
				<li>
					<strong class="text-foreground">Entering competitions before understanding the scoring:</strong>
					This is the single most preventable mistake. Knowing the game rules is not the same as knowing
					the scoring system. Read the scoring matrix for every format before playing competitively.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="text-foreground">Confusing short-term losses for skill deficit:</strong>
					In skill games, a run of losses is not necessarily evidence that you are playing poorly — it
					may simply be normal variance. Keep records over 20–30 sessions to get a meaningful picture
					of your performance level. Do not overhaul your strategy after two or three losses.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="text-foreground">Playing too many games simultaneously:</strong>
					On platforms that allow multiple concurrent contests, spreading attention across three or four
					games at once reduces the quality of decisions in each. Limit yourself to one game at a time
					until your performance in that game is consistently strong enough that it requires less
					deliberate attention.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="text-foreground">Entering large tournaments too early:</strong>
					Large player pools mean high variance and typically the best players dominate the prize
					structure. Beginners entering large tournaments experience most of the downside risk with
					little of the upside potential. Start small and build your way up.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="text-foreground">Neglecting the meta-game:</strong>
					In any competitive format, there is a meta-game — the strategic landscape defined by how
					most players play. Understanding the meta allows you to make differential selections or
					decisions that outperform the average player without needing to outperform on raw skill
					alone. This is particularly relevant in fantasy sports, where differential team selection
					can outperform even highly skilled conventional selections.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="mt-8 max-w-4xl rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-6">
			<h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-foreground md:text-3xl">Responsible Play in Skill Games</h2>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
				Even in skill games — where your decisions genuinely affect outcomes — the structure of competitive
				gaming carries risks that require responsible management. The fact that skill matters does not mean
				losses are always avoidable or that consistent profitability is guaranteed for every player.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
				Responsible skill gaming starts with an honest assessment of your skill level relative to the
				field you are entering. If you are losing more sessions than winning across a sample of 20 or
				more games at a given stakes level, that level is beyond your current skill. Step down to lower
				stakes until performance improves consistently. This is not giving up — it is the correct
				bankroll management response to an honest performance assessment.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
				Set time limits per session, not just monetary limits. Extended sessions produce fatigue-driven
				errors that impair decision-making quality in ways that are hard to detect in the moment. A 45-minute
				session with full concentration will consistently outperform a 3-hour session where the last
				90 minutes are played below optimal cognitive level. Quality of play matters more than volume
				of play for skill improvement and for financial outcomes.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
				If at any point you notice that gaming sessions are becoming compulsive, that you are playing
				to recover losses rather than for enjoyment, or that gaming is affecting your financial stability
				or daily routines, stop and seek support. Our
				<a href="/responsible-gaming" class="text-primary underline-offset-4 hover:underline">responsible gaming page</a>
				has practical self-assessment tools, deposit and time limit guidance, and contacts for support
				organisations available in India. Gaming — including skilled, competitive gaming — should
				enhance your leisure time, not compromise your wellbeing.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
